<div class="flex h-full min-h-0 flex-col">
  <.breadcrumb segments={[
    %{label: "Admin", navigate: ~p"/admin"},
    %{label: "Orgs", navigate: nil}
  ]} />
  <%!-- Header. Chrome uses semantic token slots only — `base-300` for the
       divider (border), `base-content/60` for the muted eyebrow, `primary`
       for the single CTA. No raw hex anywhere on this surface. --%>
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between border-b border-edge px-4 py-3">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <h1 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-base-content/60">
        Admin · Orgs
      </h1>
      <span class="badge badge-ghost badge-sm">{length(@orgs)}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
      <button
        type="button"
        phx-click="open_create_sheet"
        class="btn btn-sm btn-primary gap-2"
        data-role="open-create-org"
      >
        <span class="hero-plus size-4"></span> New org
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="flex-1 min-h-0 overflow-y-auto p-4">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-4">
      <div :if={@orgs == []} class="text-sm text-base-content/40">
        No orgs yet.
      </div>

      <div :if={@orgs != []} class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="table table-sm" data-role="org-list">
          <thead>
            <tr class="text-base-content/60">
              <th>Name</th>
              <th>Slug</th>
              <th>Tier</th>
              <th>Scope rail</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr :for={org <- @orgs} data-role="org-row" data-org-slug={org.slug}>
              <%!-- Swatch `shrink-0`, name `min-w-0 truncate`: a marker must
                   never win space from the title it annotates, and at a long
                   org name in a narrow admin table that is exactly what it
                   will try to do. The swatch is rendered from the org's own
                   hue, not from a class, because a class for a runtime org
                   is the defect this task exists to kill. --%>
              <td class="font-medium text-base-content">
                <div class="flex min-w-0 items-center gap-2">
                  <span
                    :if={is_integer(org.color_hue)}
                    class="size-3 shrink-0 rounded-full"
                    style={"background-color:#{org_fill(org)}"}
                    data-role="org-swatch"
                    data-org-hue={org.color_hue}
                    title={"Hue #{org.color_hue}"}
                  >
                  </span>
                  <span
                    :if={is_nil(org.color_hue)}
                    class="size-3 shrink-0 rounded-full border border-dashed border-edge"
                    data-role="org-swatch-empty"
                    title="No colour set"
                  >
                  </span>
                  <span class="min-w-0 truncate">{org.name}</span>
                </div>
              </td>
              <td class="font-mono text-xs text-base-content/60">{org.slug}</td>
              <td class="text-xs text-base-content/60">
                {if root_org?(org), do: "Root", else: "Tenant"}
              </td>
              <td>
                <span
                  :if={on_scope_rail?(org)}
                  class="badge badge-ghost badge-sm"
                  data-role="scope-rail-yes"
                >
                  On rail
                </span>
                <%!-- `scope-rail-no` used to mean "not on the rail, needs a
                     follow-up code ship". Stage 2 made the first half false
                     and this stage makes the second half false, so the badge
                     now carries the one warning that survives: an org with no
                     hue is on the rail but renders unstyled. The ARCHIVED
                     state is a different fact and keeps its own badge rather
                     than being folded into this one. --%>
                <span
                  :if={is_nil(org.color_hue)}
                  class="badge badge-warning badge-sm badge-outline"
                  data-role="scope-rail-no"
                  title="No hue set, so this org's rail chip renders unstyled. Set one and it is live on the next page load."
                >
                  No colour
                </span>
                <span
                  :if={not on_scope_rail?(org)}
                  class="badge badge-ghost badge-sm badge-outline"
                  data-role="scope-rail-archived"
                  title="Archived orgs are excluded from the scope rail."
                >
                  Archived
                </span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%!-- Create sheet — the unified `<.sheet>` (bottom drawer on mobile,
       side/centred panel on desktop). Backdrop-tap, Escape and focus
       management come from the component; nothing is hand-rolled here.
       `:if={@show_create_sheet}` rather than a permanently-mounted sheet so
       the notice copy and helper text can't leak into list-level assertions.

       The submit button lives in the `:actions` slot (the slot's documented
       purpose: Cancel plus a primary CTA) and reaches the form in the body
       through the HTML5 `form="create-org-form"` cross-form attribute — the
       same association already used by the chat composer's upload send
       button. --%>
  <.sheet
    :if={@show_create_sheet}
    id="create-org-sheet"
    open={true}
    placement={:bottom}
    on_close="close_create_sheet"
  >
    <:title>New org</:title>

    <.form
      for={@org_form}
      id="create-org-form"
      phx-change="validate_org"
      phx-submit="create_org"
      class="form-fields space-y-4"
      data-role="create-org-form"
    >
      <%!-- Name --%>
      <div>
        <label class="label label-text text-xs font-medium" for="org_name">Name *</label>
        <input
          type="text"
          id="org_name"
          name="org[name]"
          value={@org_form[:name].value}
          required
          placeholder="Cyberdyne"
          class="input input-bordered w-full"
        />
        <p
          :for={msg <- Enum.map(@org_form[:name].errors, &translate_error/1)}
          class="mt-1 text-xs text-error"
          data-role="org-name-error"
        >
          {msg}
        </p>
      </div>

      <%!-- Slug. The format rule is shown up front rather than discovered by
           rejection; the rule itself is NOT re-implemented here — the
           messages below are whatever `Org.changeset/2` produced. --%>
      <div>
        <label class="label label-text text-xs font-medium" for="org_slug">Slug *</label>
        <input
          type="text"
          id="org_slug"
          name="org[slug]"
          value={@org_form[:slug].value}
          required
          placeholder="cyberdyne"
          class="input input-bordered w-full font-mono"
        />
        <p
          :for={msg <- Enum.map(@org_form[:slug].errors, &translate_error/1)}
          class="mt-1 text-xs text-error"
          data-role="org-slug-error"
        >
          {msg}
        </p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-base-content/60" data-role="org-slug-hint">
          Lowercase, starts with a letter, then letters, digits, hyphens or underscores
          (<span class="font-mono">^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*$</span>). Must be unique.
        </p>
      </div>

      <%!-- COLOUR. A hue RING, not a hex field. A hex field cannot say
           "that yellow is invisible on white" and cannot say "that blue is
           Wayne-Tech's"; both failures are silent and both are found by
           someone else, later, on the scope rail.

           The ring is 72 server-rendered stops. The number input beside it
           is the real form field and carries the value, so the control is
           operable from a keyboard — a ring of click targets on its own is
           not. --%>
      <div>
        <label class="label label-text text-xs font-medium" for="org_color_hue">Colour</label>

        <div class="flex flex-col items-start gap-4 sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 200 200"
            class="size-44 shrink-0"
            role="group"
            aria-label="Org hue ring"
            data-role="org-hue-ring"
          >
            <path
              :for={stop <- ring_stops()}
              d={stop.path}
              stroke={stop.color}
              stroke-width="17"
              fill="none"
              class="cursor-pointer"
              phx-click="pick_hue"
              phx-value-hue={stop.hue}
              data-role="hue-stop"
              data-hue={stop.hue}
            >
              <title>Hue {stop.hue}</title>
            </path>

            <%!-- Notches sit OUTSIDE the band, at the hues already in use.
                 Read from the orgs list, never a hardcoded table: the whole
                 point of this task is that the set of orgs is data. --%>
            <path
              :for={org <- @orgs}
              :if={is_integer(org.color_hue)}
              d={ring_notch(org.color_hue)}
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              class="text-base-content/50"
              data-role="hue-notch"
              data-org-slug={org.slug}
              data-hue={org.color_hue}
            >
              <title>{org.name} — hue {org.color_hue}</title>
            </path>

            <g :if={@picked_hue}>
              <circle
                cx={elem(ring_handle(@picked_hue), 0)}
                cy={elem(ring_handle(@picked_hue), 1)}
                r="11"
                fill={@preview.ramp.fill}
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2.5"
                class="text-base-100"
                data-role="hue-handle"
                data-hue={@picked_hue}
              />
            </g>
          </svg>

          <div class="min-w-0 flex-1 space-y-2">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <input
                type="number"
                id="org_color_hue"
                name="org[color_hue]"
                value={@org_form[:color_hue].value}
                min="0"
                max="359"
                inputmode="numeric"
                placeholder="none"
                class="input input-bordered w-28 font-mono"
                data-role="org-color-hue-input"
              />
              <span class="text-xs text-base-content/60">degrees, 0–359</span>
              <button
                :if={@picked_hue}
                type="button"
                phx-click="clear_hue"
                class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs"
                data-role="clear-hue"
              >
                Clear
              </button>
            </div>

            <p
              :for={msg <- Enum.map(@org_form[:color_hue].errors, &translate_error/1)}
              class="text-xs text-error"
              data-role="org-color-hue-error"
            >
              {msg}
            </p>

            <p :if={is_nil(@picked_hue)} class="text-xs text-base-content/60">
              Optional. With no colour the org still gets a scope-rail chip — it just
              renders unstyled until a hue is set.
            </p>

            <%!-- WARNS, never blocks. Two orgs may legitimately want adjacent
                 colours, and a control that refuses would be wrong more often
                 than the operator is. --%>
            <div
              :if={@hue_conflicts != []}
              class="rounded-lg border border-warning/40 bg-warning/10 p-2 text-xs text-base-content"
              data-role="hue-proximity-warning"
            >
              <p class="flex items-center gap-1.5 font-medium">
                <span class="hero-exclamation-triangle size-4 text-warning"></span>
                Close to a colour already in use
              </p>
              <p :for={conflict <- @hue_conflicts} class="mt-1 text-base-content/70">
                {conflict.org.name} is at hue {conflict.org.color_hue},
                <span class="font-mono">{conflict.distance}°</span>
                away. You can still create this org.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%!-- PREVIEW. TWO grounds, and that is the whole point of it.
           `--color-base-100` is `oklch(100% 0 0)` in light and `#171b25` in
           dark, which are 17.21:1 apart, while clearing AA (4.5:1) as text
           on BOTH would need the grounds to be at least 4.5² = 20.25 apart.
           17.21 < 20.25, so no single colour value can do it — the answer is
           a PAIR, and a one-ground preview lets an operator approve, on a
           dark screen, a colour nobody working in light can read.

           Every figure is computed server-side in `validate_org` by
           `ScopeColor.derive/2`, never in JS, and every figure is printed
           with the ground it was measured against. That is not pedantry:
           the sxi label reads 5.10 against bare white and 4.56 against its
           own 12% tint over white, and only the second is the surface the
           text actually sits on. A bare number here is not interpretable. --%>
      <div :if={@preview} class="space-y-2" data-role="org-color-preview">
        <p class="text-xs font-medium text-base-content/60">Preview</p>

        <div class="grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div
            :for={ground <- @preview.grounds}
            class="rounded-lg border border-edge p-3"
            style={ground.panel_style}
            data-role="preview-ground"
            data-ground={ground.key}
          >
            <%!-- One chip per surface the solver solved: the chat pane
                 (base-100) and the scope rail (base-200). Each is painted
                 with the exact bytes it was measured on, its surface, its
                 OPAQUE tint and its own label, so nothing here composites
                 in the browser and the chip an operator looks at is the
                 chip the figure below describes. --%>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
              <div
                :for={cell <- ground.cells}
                class="rounded-lg p-1.5"
                style={"background-color:#{cell.color}"}
                title={cell.surface}
                data-role="preview-surface"
                data-ground={ground.key}
                data-surface={cell.surface}
              >
                <div
                  class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full px-2.5 py-1"
                  style={"background-color:#{cell.tint}"}
                >
                  <span
                    class="size-2 rounded-full"
                    style={"background-color:#{@preview.ramp.fill}"}
                  >
                  </span>
                  <span
                    class="text-xs font-medium"
                    style={"color:#{cell.label}"}
                    data-role="preview-chip-label"
                  >
                    {if @org_form[:name].value in [nil, ""],
                      do: "New org",
                      else: @org_form[:name].value}
                  </span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <%!-- The WORST cell, named. Each surface has its own tint and
                 its own label, so there are two cells per theme. Printing
                 the better one, or printing both, invites an operator to
                 read the number they like. --%>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px]" style={"color:#{ground.text_color}"}>
              <span data-role="preview-contrast" data-ground={ground.key}>
                {format_contrast(ground.worst.contrast)}:1
              </span>
              <span class="opacity-70">worst: {ground.worst.cell}</span>
            </p>
            <p class="font-mono text-[11px]" style={"color:#{ground.text_color}"}>
              <span :if={ground.worst.contrast >= 4.5} class="opacity-70">
                {ground.name} · worst cell clears AA 4.5:1
              </span>
              <span
                :if={ground.worst.contrast < 4.5}
                class="font-semibold"
                data-role="preview-aa-fail"
                data-ground={ground.key}
              >
                {ground.name} · below AA 4.5:1 here
              </span>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <%!-- This copy used to tell the operator that a figure under 4.5 was
             expected, because the solver and this panel read different cell
             tables. Task 01a09287 stage 5 made `ScopeColor.cells/1` the one
             enumeration, and task 01a09333-d374 gave each surface its own
             opaque tint and its own label, solved against that tint alone.
             Every hue on the circle clears 4.5 with margin in both themes,
             so an apology here would teach an operator to accept a failing
             figure as normal.

             The six shipped orgs are NOT solved through this path yet: they
             keep their hand-authored `app.css` tokens until the retrofit in
             task 01a09333-d374. --%>
        <p class="text-[11px] text-base-content/60" data-role="preview-worst-cell-note">
          Each theme shows the chip on the chat pane (base-100) and on the scope rail
          (base-200). Each surface gets its own solid tint and its own label, solved
          against that tint with margin for a one-byte rounding difference, and the
          figure is the worse of the two. Every hue clears 4.5 in both themes; if one
          does not, that is a bug in the solver and not a reason to pick again.
        </p>

        <%!-- The solved values, in mono because they are machine values an
             operator may paste into a bug report. `fill` stays first: it is
             what the ring's hue test reads out of this block. --%>
        <div
          class="rounded-lg bg-base-200 p-3 font-mono text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-base-content/60"
          data-role="org-color-derived"
        >
          <div>fill {@preview.ramp.fill}</div>
          <div :for={ground <- @preview.grounds}>
            <div :for={cell <- ground.cells} data-role="org-color-derived-cell">
              {ground.key} {cell.surface}: tint {cell.tint} · label {cell.label}
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%!-- Parentage is derived, not asked for — see the moduledoc. Say so,
           so its absence reads as a decision rather than a missing field. --%>
      <p class="text-xs text-base-content/60" data-role="org-parent-note">
        New orgs are created as tenants of the root org. There is exactly one root org and it
        already exists, so there is nothing to choose here.
      </p>
    </.form>

    <:actions>
      <button type="button" phx-click="close_create_sheet" class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm">
        Cancel
      </button>
      <button
        type="submit"
        form="create-org-form"
        class="btn btn-primary btn-sm"
        data-role="submit-create-org"
      >
        Create org
      </button>
    </:actions>
  </.sheet>
</div>
